{
  "_doc": "Pinned local runtime and model artifacts (docs/EMBEDDINGS.md is the evidence). Every artifact is downloaded on demand from its url (the model from a fixed Hugging Face commit), checked against its size and SHA-256 (GitHub's release digest; Hugging Face's x-linked-etag) and never run unverified: an archive's files are hashed into a manifest at install and re-checked, with the model file, before every launch (a file whose size and modification time did not change since its last check is not hashed again). Users override a runtime or model in sidecar.runtimes.file (same shape; merged per runtime and per model, null removes). Names (runtime, version, variants, artifacts, models) are plain file names: they name directories. Runtime args are templated: {model} the model file, {host} the loopback address, {port} a free port, {threads} the CPU threads (tuned at first start), {slots} and {slot_context} the parallel slots and each slot's tokens (the per-input context embedders get), {context} their product. The API key goes in key_env, never on the command line; the child gets only the env_allow variables of the app's environment, plus key_env and its variant's env. Windows ships exactly one managed build, vulkan: it is installed, verified and loaded automatically and there is no cpu or cuda build and no variant picker. Its requires field is a preflight: the app creates a Vulkan instance and enumerates a physical device before downloading anything, a machine without one gets an actionable unavailable status instead of a 342,039,075 B download, and any requires value other than vulkan (or empty) is rejected by catalog validation. Linux dev/tests keep the cpu build. A platform maps variant names to builds (sidecar.variant names one, or auto). auto still supports several GPU builds: when the platform has a cpu build, a GPU build is kept only when it passes the canary and beats cpu by sidecar.auto.min_speedup; without a cpu build each auto candidate is installed, started and canary-checked and the first that passes serves (no cpu rate, no comparison, no fallback). canary is the fixed sentence of the GPU check; tune_text is the text thread tuning and the auto probe embed (varied per input so no prompt cache hides the cost). A model's canary_vector is the pinned embedding of canary (768 values, used verbatim: never regenerated or rescaled) and canary_sentence_sha256 names the sentence it belongs to; the GPU check uses it first, so no cpu build is downloaded on Windows. The vector is not secret and is never logged. local_embedder pins the runtime and model the app writes to the owner's provider file on first start (the model record carries its complete embedding spec and chars_per_token); a custom catalog that replaces that model must update or remove the pin, or validation fails.",
